Example B1
==========

Geant4's first basic example, ported to this GPU transport, file for file.

It is here for one reason: it is the calibration. Geant4 11.1.1 was run on the same geometry
for 2,000,000 events and reported a dose of 427.385 +/- 0.870 picogray per 10,000 events; this
example reports 427.499, which is 0.09 sigma away. Every claim in docs/RESULT.md rests on that
number, and it is computed by this code and no other.

    exampleB1                    interactive: runs init_vis.mac, opens the viewer
    exampleB1 run1.mac           batch, from a macro
    exampleB1 run2.mac           batch, 10,000 events
    exampleB1 -n 2000000         batch, an event count and nothing else


The geometry
------------

Unchanged from Geant4's:

- **World**, 24 x 24 x 36 cm of G4_AIR
- **Envelope**, 20 x 20 x 30 cm of G4_WATER, inside it
- **Shape 1**, a cone of G4_A-150_TISSUE, r 2 -> 4 cm, half-length 3 cm, at (0, 2, -7) cm
- **Shape 2**, a trapezoid of G4_BONE_COMPACT_ICRU, 12 x 10 -> 12 x 16 cm, half-length 3 cm,
  at (0, -1, 7) cm - the scoring volume

The primary is a 6 MeV gamma along +z, spread uniformly over 80% of the envelope's transverse
size, starting at z = -15 cm. The production range cut is 0.7 mm.


The files
---------

The same layout and the same class names as Geant4's B1, because a project you already know
how to read is worth more than a tidier one you do not:

    exampleB1.cc                     main(): run manager, physics list, actions, session
    include/ + src/
      DetectorConstruction           the geometry above, and the sensitive detector
      ActionInitialization           creates the actions, in Build() and BuildForMaster()
      PrimaryGeneratorAction         the gun
      RunAction                      accumulates edep, prints the dose and its rms
      EventAction                    per-event accumulation
      SteppingAction                 filters to the scoring volume, adds the deposit
    exampleB1.in, run1.mac, run2.mac, init_vis.mac, vis.mac, init.mac
    CMakeLists.txt                   cmake build, as a Geant4 example has
    build.bat                        the direct nvcc build the pipeline uses


Three differences from Geant4's B1
----------------------------------

**DetectorConstruction has a ConstructSDandField().** Geant4's B1 does not: it gets its dose
purely from the stepping action, which decides per step whether the step was in the scoring
volume. Here the steps happen inside a device kernel, so that decision has to be made before
the run: a volume with a sensitive detector gets a scorer slot and the kernels accumulate into
it. The stepping action still runs and still reads the deposit off the step it is handed.

**A step is an aggregate.** `UserSteppingAction` is called once per event and per volume with a
sensitive detector, with that event's total in that volume - not once per step. B1's stepping
action adds up what it is given, and the sum over an event of the per-step deposits is the
event's deposit, so the answer is exactly right. An action that did something non-additive
with a step would not be. `G4Step::IsAggregated()` returns true so that such an action can
detect it; the full story is at the top of `src/g4/G4Step.hh`.

**The proton runs are commented out.** Geant4's run1.mac, run2.mac and exampleB1.in each
follow the gamma run with 210 MeV protons. This transport carries gamma, e- and e+; hadron
transport is not implemented, and `/run/beamOn` refuses a species it cannot transport rather
than treating a proton as a gamma. The lines are left in the macros, commented, so that the
difference is visible.

Two smaller ones, both consequences of running on a GPU rather than a CPU:

- `PrimaryGeneratorAction::GeneratePrimaries` is called **once per run**, not once per event.
  Geant4's B1 draws two random numbers there to spread the beam; here the spread is set on the
  gun as a distribution (`SetBeamCrossSectionRectangular`) and the device samples it per
  event. See `src/g4/G4UserActions.hh`.
- `G4UserTrackingAction` and `G4UserStackingAction` compile but are never called, and say so
  when registered.


Building
--------

With cmake, the way a Geant4 example is built:

    cmake -S . -B build
    cmake --build build --config Release

Or directly, which is what `build_all.bat` runs:

    build.bat

Both produce `exampleB1.exe`. There is also a `GNUmakefile`, as in Geant4's B1 - Geant4's
delegates to `$(G4INSTALL)/config/binmake.gmk` and there is no such install here, so this one
runs the same two nvcc invocations itself:

    make            build
    make run        build and run exampleB1.in
    make clean

Object files go to `out/B1` at the root of the tree, not into a directory here - a project that
can import CAD meshes should not have a pile of files called `*.obj` next to its source meaning
something else entirely.


The file set
------------

Everything Geant4's `examples/basic/B1` has, with three files that are not identical in kind:

| file | here |
|---|---|
| `exampleB1.cc`, `include/`, `src/` | the same six classes, with the same responsibilities |
| `exampleB1.in`, `run1.mac`, `run2.mac`, `init_vis.mac`, `vis.mac` | the same macros; `run2.mac`'s proton section is commented, because nothing here transports a hadron yet |
| `CMakeLists.txt`, `GNUmakefile` | both build this project rather than linking Geant4 |
| `README`, `History` | `History` tracks the port's B1; Geant4's own is in its source tree |
| `exampleB1.out` | this port's output for `exampleB1.in`, generated by running it - not Geant4's |
| `tsg_offscreen.mac` | offscreen rendering, through this viewer's own export command rather than ToolsSG |

`DetectorConstruction::ConstructSDandField` is the one addition: the device needs the scored
volume before the run begins. The comment at that function says why.



What it prints
--------------

`exampleB1.out` holds the output of `exampleB1 exampleB1.in` as a reference - 10,000 6 MeV
gammas. It was produced by running it, not written by hand, so the timings in it are one
machine's on one day and will not reproduce; the dose will, to within the rms printed beside
it. Geant4's own `exampleB1.out` is 970 lines and this one is 23, almost all of the difference
being physics-table printout and per-100-event progress that a batched GPU run has nothing to
say about.

The dose block is Geant4's; everything after the marked line in `RunAction::EndOfRunAction` is
this project's - the comparison against Geant4 11.1.1 and the throughput.

The rms deserves a note. It is this run's own: the second moment of the per-event energy
deposit distribution, accumulated exactly as Geant4's B1 accumulates it, through
`EventAction` and `RunAction::AddEdep`. That it lands on 0.870 picogray per 10,000 events -
the same as Geant4's - is a second, independent check on the physics, and a stronger one than
the mean, because two transports can agree on an average while disagreeing about the shape of
the distribution behind it.
